inking": "Let me solve this geometry problem. I'll work through it carefully.\n\nWe have triangle ABC with AB=3, AC=5, BC=7. E is the reflection of A over BC. Line BE meets the circumcircle of ABC again at D. I is the incenter of triangle ABD. Find cos²∠AEI = m/n, compute m+n.\n\nLet me set up coordinates. Place B at origin, C at (7,0). Then A is at some point.\n\nAB=3, AC=5, BC=7.\nA = (x,y) with x²+y²=9 and (x-7)²+y²=25.\nx²+y²=9, x²-14x+49+y²=25.\n9-14x+49=25 → -14x+58=25 → 14x=33 → x=33/14.\ny²=9-(33/14)²=9-1089/196=(1764-1089)/196=675/196.\ny=√675/14=15√3/14.\n\nSo A=(33/14, 15√3/14).\n\nE is reflection of A over BC (the x-axis): E=(33/14, -15√3/14).\n\nLine BE: B=(0,0), E=(33/14, -15√3/14). Direction: (33, -15√3). Parametrize: points on BE are t·(33, -15√3) for scalar t (using direction vector). Actually let me use B + s·(E-B) = s·(33/14, -15√3/14). Let me just use direction (33, -15√3) and parametrize as (33t, -15√3 t).\n\nCircumcircle of ABC: passes through B=(0,0), C=(7,0), A=(33/14, 15√3/14).\n\nGeneral circle: x²+y²+Dx+Ey+F=0. Through B(0,0): F=0. Through C(7,0): 49+7D=0 → D=-7. Through A: (33/14)²+(15√3/14)²-7·(33/14)+E·(15√3/14)=0.\n(1089+675)/196 - 231/14 + 15√3 E/14 = 0.\n1764/196 - 231/14 + 15√3 E/14 = 0.\n9 - 231/14 + 15√3 E/14 = 0.\n126/14 - 231/14 + 15√3 E/14 = 0.\n-105/14 + 15√3 E/14 = 0 → 15√3 E = 105 → E = 105/(15√3) = 7/√3 = 7√3/3.\n\nSo circle: x²+y²-7x+(7√3/3)y=0.\n\nNow find D: intersection of line (33t, -15√3 t) with circle, other than B (t=0).\n(33t)²+(-15√3 t)²-7(33t)+(7√3/3)(-15√3 t)=0.\n1089t²+675t²-231t+(7√3/3)(-15√3)t=0.\n(1089+675)t²-231t-7·15·t=0.  [since (7√3/3)(15√3)=7·15·3/3=105, so -105t]\n1764t²-231t-105t=0.\n1764t²-336t=0.\nt(1764t-336)=0.\nt=336/1764=336/1764. Simplify: gcd(336,1764). 1764=4·441=4·21². 336=16·21. 336/1764=16/84=4/21.\n\nSo t=4/21. D=(33·4/21, -15√3·4/21)=(132/21, -60√3/21)=(44/7, -20√3/7).\n\nLet me verify D is on circle. D=(44/7, -20√3/7).\nx²+y²=(1936+1200)/49=3136/49=64.\n-7x=-7·44/7=-44.\n(7√3/3)y=(7√3/3)(-20√3/7)=-20·3/3=-20.\nTotal: 64-44-20=0. ✓\n\nNow triangle ABD. A=(33/14,15√3/14), B=(0,0), D=(44/7,-20√3/7).\n\nLet me compute side lengths.\nAB=3 (given).\nBD: |D|=√(64)=8. So BD=8.\nAD: A-D=(33/14-44/7, 15√3/14+20√3/7)=(33/14-88/14, 15√3/14+40√3/14)=(-55/14, 55√3/14).\n|AD|=55/14·√(1+3)=55/14·2=110/14=55/7.\n\nSo triangle ABD has sides AB=3, BD=8, AD=55/7.\n\nCheck: 3+55/7=21/7+55/7=76/7 > 8=56/7 ✓. 3+8=11 > 55/7≈7.86 ✓.\n\nIncenter I of triangle ABD. I = (a·A_vertex + b·B_vertex + c·C_vertex)/(a+b+c) where a,b,c are opposite side lengths.\n\nWait, standard: incenter = (a·A + b·B + c·C)/(a+b+c) where a = side opposite A = BD = 8, b = side opposite B = AD = 55/7, c = side opposite D = AB = 3.\n\nVertices: A=(33/14,15√3/14), B=(0,0), D=(44/7,-20√3/7).\n\nI = (8·A + (55/7)·B + 3·D)/(8+55/7+3).\nDenominator: 8+55/7+3 = 11+55/7 = 77/7+55/7 = 132/7.\n\nNumerator x: 8·(33/14) + (55/7)·0 + 3·(44/7) = 264/14 + 132/7 = 132/7 + 132/7 = 264/7.\nNumerator y: 8·(15√3/14) + 0 + 3·(-20√3/7) = 120√3/14 - 60√3/7 = 60√3/7 - 60√3/7 = 0.\n\nInteresting! I_y = 0. So I is on the x-axis (line BC).\n\nI_x = (264/7)/(132/7) = 264/132 = 2.\n\nSo I = (2, 0).\n\nNow we need angle AEI. E=(33/14, -15√3/14), A=(33/14, 15√3/14), I=(2,0).\n\nNote A and E are reflections, so AE is vertical: A and E have same x-coordinate 33/14. AE is the segment from (33/14, 15√3/14) to (33/14, -15√3/14), vertical line x=33/14.\n\nAngle AEI is the angle at E in triangle AEI, i.e., ∠AEI, the angle between EA and EI.\n\nEA = A - E = (0, 30√3/14) = (0, 15√3/7). Direction: straight up (positive y).\nEI = I - E = (2 - 33/14, 0 - (-15√3/14)) = (28/14 - 33/14, 15√3/14) = (-5/14, 15√3/14).\n\ncos(∠AEI) = (EA · EI)/(|EA||EI|).\nEA·EI = 0·(-5/14) + (15√3/7)·(15√3/14) = (15√3·15√3)/(7·14) = (225·3)/98 = 675/98.\n|EA| = 15√3/7.\n|EI| = √((5/14)² + (15√3/14)²) = (1/14)√(25 + 675) = (1/14)√700 = (1/14)·10√7 = 10√7/14 = 5√7/7.\n\ncos(∠AEI) = (675/98)/((15√3/7)(5√7/7)) = (675/98)/((75√21)/49) = (675/98)·(49/(75√21)) = (675·49)/(98·75√21) = (675·49)/(7350√21).\n\nSimplify: 675/75 = 9. So = (9·49)/(98√21) = 441/(98√21) = 441/(98√21).\n441/98 = 441/98. gcd: 441=9·49, 98=2·49. So 441/98 = 9/2.\ncos(∠AEI) = 9/(2√21) = 9√21/42 = 3√21/14.\n\ncos²(∠AEI) = 9·21/196 = 189/196.\n\nWait let me double check: (9/(2√21))² = 81/(4·21) = 81/84 = 27/28.\n\nHmm let me recompute. 9/(2√21), squared = 81/(4·21)=81/84=27/28.\n\nLet me recheck. cos = 3√21/14? (3√21/14)² = 9·21/196 = 189/196. That's different from 27/28 = 189/196. Oh wait! 27/28 = 27·7/196 = 189/196. Yes! Same thing.\n\nSo cos² = 189/196. gcd(189,196): 189=27·7=3³·7, 196=4·49=2²·7². gcd=7. 189/7=27, 196/7=28. So cos²=27/28.\n\nm=27, n=28, m+n=55.\n\nLet me double-check the incenter computation and the angle.\n\nActually, let me recheck the incenter formula. For triangle with vertices A, B, D and opposite sides a (opposite A, =BD), b (opposite B, =AD), c (opposite D, =AB):\nIncenter = (a·A + b·B + c·D)/(a+b+c).\na=BD=8, b=AD=55/7, c=AB=3. ✓\n\nI = (8A + (55/7)B + 3D)/(8+55/7+3).\nB=(0,0) so middle term is 0.\n8A = 8·(33/14, 15√3/14) = (264/14, 120√3/14) = (132/7, 60√3/7).\n3D = 3·(44/7, -20√3/7) = (132/7, -60√3/7).\nSum = (264/7, 0). ✓\nDenominator = 8 + 55/7 + 3 = 132/7. ✓\nI = (264/7)/(132/7) = 2, and y=0. I=(2,0). ✓\n\nGreat, very clean.\n\nNow angle AEI at E.\nE = (33/14, -15√3/14).\nA = (33/14, 15√3/14).\nI = (2, 0) = (28/14, 0).\n\nEA = A - E = (0, 30√3/14) = (0, 15√3/7). Points straight up. ✓\nEI = I - E = (28/14 - 33/14, 0 + 15√3/14) = (-5/14, 15√3/14). ✓\n\nDot product: 0·(-5/14) + (15√3/7)(15√3/14) = 225·3/(98) = 675/98. ✓\n|EA| = 15√3/7. ✓\n|EI|² = (5/14)² + (15√3/14)² = (25 + 675)/196 = 700/196 = 100/28 = 25/7.\n|EI| = 5/√7 = 5√7/7. ✓\n\ncos = (675/98)/((15√3/7)(5√7/7)) = (675/98)/(75√21/49) = 675·49/(98·75√21) = 9·49/(98√21) = 441/(98√21) = 9/(2√21).\n\ncos² = 81/(4·21) = 81/84 = 27/28. ✓\n\nm=27, n=28, gcd(27,28)=1. ✓\n\nm+n = 55.\n\nThe answer is 55.", "tool_calls": [], "created_at": 1786666366}
